For my final project, I created an R package called mpgInsights. This package is designed to help users easily analyze and visualize the mpg dataset, which is included in the ggplot2 package. The goal of this project was not just to write code but to fully design, document, and publish a working R package that meets CRAN-like standards and is accessible to users through GitHub. In this post, I’ll explain the rationale behind the package design, provide usage instructions, and describe how AI tools supported my development process.

The mpgInsights package currently includes two core functions. The first, summarize_mpg(), provides summary statistics by calculating the average city and highway miles per gallon (MPG) for each vehicle class. This function uses the dplyr package to group and summarize the data cleanly. The second function, plot_mpg(), creates a scatter plot comparing city and highway MPG values, with vehicle classes distinguished by color. This visual allows users to easily spot fuel efficiency trends across different types of vehicles. Both functions are written with clarity and minimal dependencies to ensure that the package is lightweight and user-friendly.

The package is built using standard R package structure, including a DESCRIPTION file for metadata, an R/ folder for function scripts, a man/ directory with .Rd documentation files, and a vignettes/ folder containing a detailed introduction in R Markdown format. I also created an Rmd file inside the man/ folder to explain the implementation details of each function in plain language—meeting the project requirement for long-form documentation. Unit tests were added using the testthat framework, ensuring that functions return expected outputs and behave reliably. Finally, the package is licensed under the MIT License, making it open-source and reusable.

To install the package, users can run devtools::install_github("NadeemAlBarwani/mpgInsights") in their R console. After loading the package with library(mpgInsights), they can call summarize_mpg() to view fuel economy averages or plot_mpg() to generate a scatter plot of city versus highway MPG, colored by vehicle class.

An important part of this project was transparency regarding AI usage. I used ChatGPT as a support tool throughout development—mainly to assist in setting up the folder structure, drafting function templates, writing documentation, and planning tests. While AI helped with these foundational tasks, all code was manually reviewed, tested, and refined by me to ensure correctness and originality. I’ve disclosed AI involvement here and in the README file, in accordance with course requirements. This ensured that the final submission reflects both independent work and responsible use of modern tools.

In conclusion, building mpgInsights provided me with valuable experience in package development, documentation, and ethical software practices. I learned how to structure code for reusability, document it thoroughly for other users, and share it via GitHub in a professional format. I plan to continue developing this package by adding features such as filtering by manufacturer and enabling interactivity with libraries like plotly. I welcome feedback from my peers and look forward to expanding mpgInsights into a more robust fuel economy analysis toolkit.
